diff --git a/libs/slutils/NATIVE_WINDOWS_TRANSPORT.md b/libs/slutils/NATIVE_WINDOWS_TRANSPORT.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/libs/slutils/NATIVE_WINDOWS_TRANSPORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# Windows acquisition transport (P8-WINBACKEND-04)
+
+Implementation/build evidence only. No application or service has been executed. Linux retains its original process, descriptor and select paths.
+
+Windows plugin commands must use the explicit mapping `native:<Windows executable and quoted argv>`; the configured plugin name is appended as one argument. UTF-8 configuration is converted to UTF-16 and Windows argument quoting is applied. There is no shell fallback or translation of POSIX shell syntax. Existing shell scripts/configurations need explicit operator mapping to a supported native binary.
+
+CreateProcessW receives an inherited-handle whitelist: a byte-mode overlapped packet writer, cooperative stop event, synchronous stdout/stderr logging writer, and NUL stdin. The parent read endpoints are non-inheritable. A suspended child is assigned to a kill-on-close job before resume. Parent writer duplicates are closed immediately. Per-child environment variables SEISCOMP_PLUGIN_PIPE and SEISCOMP_PLUGIN_STOP encode uintptr_t-sized handles in decimal; these are bootstrap metadata, never packet bytes.
+
+Supported native child implementations are caps_plugin and fs_plugin. They call sl_native_plugin_bootstrap before acquisition. The bootstrap takes ownership of the packet handle through _open_osfhandle, duplicates it to child-local CRT FD 63, enables binary mode, clears handle inheritance, and registers that descriptor as an overlapped pipe. The temporary CRT descriptor is closed exactly once. Stop-event inheritance is also cleared. Packet helpers serialize the original header and payload under one mutex. No packet declaration, field encoding or sample algorithm is substituted.
+
+Arbitrary external plugins are unsupported until rebuilt with this bootstrap and cooperative-stop integration; merely compiling a legacy _write(63) call does not satisfy inheritance or ABI requirements. chain_plugin extensions also require separate FD 61 (commands) and FD 62 (incoming data) bindings; this single-output bootstrap does not claim that external contract.
+
+CRT file descriptors, pipe HANDLEs and Winsock SOCKETs have distinct typed endpoints. WSAPoll sees sockets only. PeekNamedPipe supplies byte-pipe readiness; overlapped operations resolve completion after CancelIoEx before stack OVERLAPPED/event/buffer lifetimes end. Result states distinguish bytes, temporary no-data, EOF, timeout and native errors. Timed writes retain a fresh timeout interval after each completed partial write, matching the upstream adapter. Unknown CRT pipe descriptors are rejected rather than sent through Winsock or silently treated as ready.
+
+Wall timestamps use signed 64-bit Unix seconds and microseconds derived from FILETIME; Timer formulas and scheduled increments remain unchanged. Blocking wait deadlines use GetTickCount64. Packet state holds partial header/payload reads until complete; sample counts are bounded before multiplication on Windows. Compile-time layout checks are source-derived engineering checks, not Linux-oracle or runtime ABI validation.
+
+Shutdown sets the separate stop event. CAPS checks it in its collection loop and bounded socket-read polling; FS checks between directory scans and during its polling wait, then flushes existing decoder channels. Existing parent shutdown timers control escalation. TerminateJobObject is forced shutdown, never a graceful-shutdown claim. Process/job ownership prevents orphaned descendants and packet writers on parent destruction. Name resolution/connect and lengthy scans can require bounded parent escalation; graceful behavior is not runtime qualified.
+
+Platform baseline is Windows 8 / Server 2012 API availability (_WIN32_WINNT=0x0602). Filesystem adapters currently use the Windows ANSI/CRT path APIs and do not establish Unicode or extended-length-path support. No dependency versions changed. Boost POSIX regex API implementation is the exact existing installed-version source with Boost Software License 1.0 included; no regex approximation was introduced.
